{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775255889-3e7XYEMYvYpPSNgTZY7H","role":"assistant","created":1775255907,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see Q4 1987 data (Oct-Dec 1987). I can see:\n- October 1987: $9,255 million accepted\n- November 1987: $9,269 million accepted  \n- December 1987: $9,286 million accepted\n\nWait, let me re-read the problem. The question asks for auction amount tenders accepted for the last calendar-year quarter of the year of the stock market crash (Black Monday). Black Monday was October 19, 1987, so the crash happened in 1987.\n\nThe last calendar-year quarter of 1987 would be Q4 1987 (October, November, December 1987).\n\nThen for the next three years:\n- Q4 1988\n- Q4 1989\n- Q4 1990\n\nSo I need:\n- Q4 1987: Oct, Nov, Dec 1987 auctions\n- Q4 1988: Oct, Nov, Dec 1988 auctions\n- Q4 1989: Oct, Nov, Dec 1989 auctions\n- Q4 1990: Oct, Nov, Dec 1990 auctions\n\nFor each Q4, I need to calculate the average of auction amount tenders accepted for 2-year Treasury notes.\n\nLet me now extract data from each year's bulletin for the Q4 data:\n\nFor Q4 1987:\n- October 1987: $9,255 million accepted\n- November 1987: $9,269 million accepted\n- December 1987: $9,286 million accepted\n\nAverage Q4 1987 = (9,255 + 9,269 + 9,286) / 3 = 27,810 / 3 = 9,270 million\n\nNow I need to find Q4 1988, Q4 1989, and Q4 1990 data. Let me check each file.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_2775feae9da2d97e","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -B2 -A8 \"Auction of 2-Year Notes\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1989_03.txt | head -100"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_f689c1b0-fc33-4f70-b393-0c344e3c8f29","role":"user","created":1775255907,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_2775feae9da2d97e","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tenders for the notes were received until 1 p.m. EDST, October 12, 1988, and totaled $16,138 million, of which $6,754 million was accepted at yields ranging from 8.72 percent, price 99.510, up to 8.75 percent, price 99.356. Tenders at the high yield were allotted 61 percent. Noncompetitive tenders were accepted in full at the average yield, 8.73 percent, price 99.459. These totaled $306 million. Competitive tenders accepted from private investors totaled $6,448 million.\n\nAuction of 2-Year Notes\n\nOn October 19 the Treasury announced that it would auction $9,000 million of 2-year notes to refund $10,904 million of notes maturing October 31, 1988, and to pay down about $1,900 million. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series AG-1990, dated October 31, 1988, due October 31, 1990, with interest payable on April 30 and October 31 until maturity. An interest rate of 8-1/4 percent was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\n\nTenders for the notes were received until 1 p.m. EDST, October 26, and totaled $25,136 million, of which $9,014 million was accepted at yields ranging from 8.31 percent, price 99.891, up to 8.34 percent, price 99.837. Tenders at the high yield were allotted 88 percent. Noncompetitive tenders were accepted in full at the average yield, 8.33 percent, price 99.855. These totaled $1,146 million. Competitive tenders accepted from private investors totaled $7,869 million.\n\nIn addition to the $9,014 million of tenders accepted in the auction process, $980 million was awarded to Federal Reserve banks as agents for foreign and international monetary authorities. An additional $639 million was accepted from Government accounts and Federal Reserve banks for their own account.\n\n52-Week Bills","annotations":{"priority":0.0}}],"structuredContent":{"stdout":"Tenders for the notes were received until 1 p.m. EDST, October 12, 1988, and totaled $16,138 million, of which $6,754 million was accepted at yields ranging from 8.72 percent, price 99.510, up to 8.75 percent, price 99.356. Tenders at the high yield were allotted 61 percent. Noncompetitive tenders were accepted in full at the average yield, 8.73 percent, price 99.459. These totaled $306 million. Competitive tenders accepted from private investors totaled $6,448 million.\n\nAuction of 2-Year Notes\n\nOn October 19 the Treasury announced that it would auction $9,000 million of 2-year notes to refund $10,904 million of notes maturing October 31, 1988, and to pay down about $1,900 million. The notes offered were Treasury Notes of Series AG-1990, dated October 31, 1988, due October 31, 1990, with interest payable on April 30 and October 31 until maturity. An interest rate of 8-1/4 percent was set after the determination as to which tenders were accepted on a yield auction basis.\n\nTenders for the notes were received until 1 p.m. EDST, October 26, and totaled $25,136 million, of which $9,014 million was accepted at yields ranging from 8.31 percent, price 99.891, up to 8.34 percent, price 99.837. Tenders at the high yield were allotted 88 percent. Noncompetitive tenders were accepted in full at the average yield, 8.33 percent, price 99.855. These totaled $1,146 million. Competitive tenders accepted from private investors totaled $7,869 million.\n\nIn addition to the $9,014 million of tenders accepted in the auction process, $980 million was awarded to Federal Reserve banks as agents for foreign and international monetary authorities. An additional $639 million was accepted from Government accounts and Federal Reserve banks for their own account.\n\n52-Week Bills","stderr":"","exit_code":0},"isError":false}}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
